  "result_paragraph": "The double-bounce hypothesis is supported: among the 60,936 OPERA-confirmed new-flood pixels our NISAR HH-darkening detector missed, 45% sit on urban or forest land cover vs 5% of the pixels it caught (8.562x enriched). Mean HH change Dec 4 -> Dec 16 was -2.72 dB (n=31,662) for flooded open land vs -0.66 dB (n=2,348) flooded urban and -0.18 dB (n=16,753) flooded forest (non-flooded controls drift ~+0.2 to +0.4 dB) — open-field water goes dark while flooded towns and trees stay near their bright baseline, so a darkening-only threshold misses exactly where people live: recall is 24.7% on open land but 3.4% in urban and 1.3% in forest. Caveat: flooded urban/forest pixels do not jump +3 dB (<=0.5% do) — double bounce cancels the darkening instead, so the existing +3 dB 'bright-flood' layer recovers only 0.01% of the misses; closing the gap needs HV/texture or land-cover-aware detection, not a brighter-than-baseline gate.",
